  "summary": "Brilliant carved wooden bird masks and banana-leaf cloaks worn by Dayak dancers seeding the farms.",
  "description": "Hudoq dancers of the Kayan and Kenyah Dayak appear as hornbill-headed birds: long carved wooden beaks crowned with feathers, bodies hidden under shredded banana leaf or bark cloaks, arms flung wide. Dancing at the start of the rice year, the masked figures drive spirits and pests from the fields and bless the seed. Some hudoq masks are stained black with soot, others painted in bright commercial colors; the flying hornbill posture links each dancer to upperworld messengers.",
